@page "/tables/simple-table/sorting"

@using ViciOne.Ui.Blazor.Components.Button
@using ViciOne.Ui.Blazor.Components.Button.Enums
@using ViciOne.Ui.Blazor.Components.Tables.SimpleTable.Components
@using ViciOne.Ui.Blazor.Components.Tables.SimpleTable.Components.Columns

<h3>Column-level sort</h3>

<DescriptionBanner>
    <code>SimpleTableTemplateColumn</code> accepts <code>SortExpression</code> with a LINQ expression to enable sorting by that key.

    Columns without a <code>SortExpression</code> are not sortable.

    Click once for ascending, again for descending.
</DescriptionBanner>

<SimpleTable Items="s_items" RowClickSelectionEnabled="false" MaximumHeight="300px">
    <Columns>
        <SimpleTableTemplateColumn Id="Key" Title="Key" SortExpression="x => x.Key">
            <CellContent>
                @context.Key
            </CellContent>
        </SimpleTableTemplateColumn>
        <SimpleTableTemplateColumn Id="Value" Title="Value" SortExpression="x => x.Value">
            <CellContent>
                @context.Value
            </CellContent>
        </SimpleTableTemplateColumn>
        <SimpleTableTemplateColumn Id="Timestamp" Title="Timestamp">
            <CellContent>
                @context.Timestamp
            </CellContent>
        </SimpleTableTemplateColumn>
    </Columns>
</SimpleTable>

<h3>Tied rows — descending is the exact reverse of ascending</h3>

<DescriptionBanner>
    Toggling a direction reorders every row, not only the key groups. Rows that tie on every sorted column keep
    the order they have in <code>Items</code>, reversed once the innermost sorting is descending.

    Sort by <code>Key</code> below: three rows share each key, and switching to descending reverses the tied
    rows inside a group as well as the groups themselves. The guarantee comes from
    <code>SimpleTable</code>'s built-in provider — a consumer-supplied <code>IItemsProvider</code> defines its
    own tie order.
</DescriptionBanner>

<SimpleTable Items="s_tiedItems" RowClickSelectionEnabled="false" MaximumHeight="300px">
    <Columns>
        <SimpleTableTemplateColumn Id="Key" Title="Key" SortExpression="x => x.Key" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>
                @context.Key
            </CellContent>
        </SimpleTableTemplateColumn>
        <SimpleTableTemplateColumn Id="Value" Title="Value" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>
                @context.Value
            </CellContent>
        </SimpleTableTemplateColumn>
    </Columns>
</SimpleTable>

<h3>Sort by more than one column</h3>

<DescriptionBanner>
    <kbd>Shift</kbd>-click a header to add its column to the sort instead of replacing it. The added column becomes the
    lowest priority, so it decides the order of the rows that tie on everything sorted before it. <kbd>Shift</kbd>-clicking
    a column that is already sorted flips its direction and keeps its priority, while a plain click drops
    every column but the clicked one.

    The rows below are the tied ones again. Sort by <code>Key</code>, then <kbd>Shift</kbd>-click <code>Value</code>: the
    groups stay put and the rows inside them line up by their source position instead of falling back to the
    tie order.
</DescriptionBanner>

<SimpleTable Items="s_tiedItems" RowClickSelectionEnabled="false" MaximumHeight="300px">
    <Columns>
        <SimpleTableTemplateColumn Id="Key" Title="Key" SortExpression="x => x.Key" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>
                @context.Key
            </CellContent>
        </SimpleTableTemplateColumn>
        <SimpleTableTemplateColumn Id="Value" Title="Value" SortExpression="x => x.Value" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>
                @context.Value
            </CellContent>
        </SimpleTableTemplateColumn>
    </Columns>
</SimpleTable>

<h3>Custom sort comparer — natural ordering</h3>

<DescriptionBanner>
    <code>SortComparer</code> accepts any <code>IComparer</code> (derive from <code>Comparer&lt;TKey&gt;</code> or
    use .NET comparers like <code>StringComparer</code>) to override how the keys of a <code>SortExpression</code>
    are compared. It applies to <code>SimpleTable</code>'s in-memory sorting only.

    Both <code>Code</code> columns below sort the same code, but the right one uses the .NET's natural-ordering comparer —
    <code>CompareInfo.GetStringComparer(CompareOptions.NumericOrdering)</code> — placing
    <code>FB2</code> before <code>FB10</code> where the default lexicographic sort does not.
</DescriptionBanner>

<SimpleTable Items="s_codeItems" RowClickSelectionEnabled="false" MaximumHeight="300px">
    <Columns>
        <SimpleTableTemplateColumn Id="Value" Title="Value" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>
                @context.Value
            </CellContent>
        </SimpleTableTemplateColumn>
        <SimpleTableTemplateColumn Id="CodeDefault" Title="Code (default sort)" SortExpression="x => x.Key" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>
                @context.Key
            </CellContent>
        </SimpleTableTemplateColumn>
        <SimpleTableTemplateColumn Id="CodeNatural"
                                   Title="Code (natural sort)"
                                   SortExpression="x => x.Key"
                                   SortComparer="s_naturalComparer"
                                   Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>
                @context.Key
            </CellContent>
        </SimpleTableTemplateColumn>
    </Columns>
</SimpleTable>

<h3>Start with a sort — an unchanging <code>SortingState</code></h3>

<DescriptionBanner>
    Pass <code>SortingState</code> from a field that is never reassigned to give the table the sort it starts
    with. The table applies it once the columns have registered and owns it from then on, because handing over
    the same instance again is not a new command — clicking a header changes the sort and nothing puts it back.
    An entry for a column the table does not show is dropped. A plain header click replaces the whole value, so
    a sort over several columns survives a stray click only as far as <kbd>Shift</kbd>-clicking rebuilds it.
</DescriptionBanner>

<SimpleTable Items="s_items" RowClickSelectionEnabled="false" SortingState="_seedSortingState" MaximumHeight="300px">
    <Columns>
        <SimpleTableTemplateColumn Id="Key" Title="Key" SortExpression="x => x.Key" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>
                @context.Key
            </CellContent>
        </SimpleTableTemplateColumn>
        <SimpleTableTemplateColumn Id="Value" Title="Value" SortExpression="x => x.Value" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>
                @context.Value
            </CellContent>
        </SimpleTableTemplateColumn>
        <SimpleTableTemplateColumn Id="Timestamp" Title="Timestamp" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>
                @context.Timestamp
            </CellContent>
        </SimpleTableTemplateColumn>
    </Columns>
</SimpleTable>

<h3>Observe changes — <code>SortingStateChanged</code></h3>

<DescriptionBanner>
    Handle <code>SortingStateChanged</code> without passing <code>SortingState</code> to watch the applied sort
    without driving it. It reports the applied sorting after every change, whatever caused it. The list below
    reports what was last raised.
</DescriptionBanner>

@if (_observedSortingState.ColumnSortings.Count > 0)
{
    <ul>
        @foreach (var columnSorting in _observedSortingState.ColumnSortings)
        {
            <li><code>@columnSorting.ColumnId</code> — @(columnSorting.Ascending ? "ascending" : "descending")</li>
        }
    </ul>
}
else
{
    <p><em>Nothing observed yet — click a column header.</em></p>
}

<SimpleTable Items="s_items" RowClickSelectionEnabled="false" SortingStateChanged="ObservedSortingStateChanged" MaximumHeight="300px">
    <Columns>
        <SimpleTableTemplateColumn Id="Key" Title="Key" SortExpression="x => x.Key" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>
                @context.Key
            </CellContent>
        </SimpleTableTemplateColumn>
        <SimpleTableTemplateColumn Id="Value" Title="Value" SortExpression="x => x.Value" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>
                @context.Value
            </CellContent>
        </SimpleTableTemplateColumn>
        <SimpleTableTemplateColumn Id="Timestamp" Title="Timestamp" SortExpression="x => x.Timestamp" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>
                @context.Timestamp
            </CellContent>
        </SimpleTableTemplateColumn>
    </Columns>
</SimpleTable>

<h3>Drive the sort from markup — <code>@("@bind-SortingState")</code></h3>

<DescriptionBanner>
    Bind <code>SortingState</code> to drive the table from a field and have every change written back into it,
    whoever made it — the buttons below or a column header. A new instance in the field is a command; the same
    instance again is not, which is what lets the table's own value flow back without starting over.

    <strong>Note:</strong> build the value in a field and only replace it when it really changes. A parent that
    produces a fresh, content-equal instance on every render makes the table re-apply on every render, and under
    a binding that never terminates — each apply reports back, each report renders the parent, each render
    builds another instance.
</DescriptionBanner>

<div class="options">
    <div class="option">
        <label>Sort order</label>
        <Button Text="Sort by Key" OnClick="SortBoundTableByKey" Size="ButtonSize.Small" />
    </div>
    <div class="option">
        <label>Sort order</label>
        <Button Text="Clear sort" OnClick="ClearBoundSorting" Size="ButtonSize.Small" />
    </div>
</div>

@if (_boundSortingState.ColumnSortings.Count > 0)
{
    <ul>
        @foreach (var columnSorting in _boundSortingState.ColumnSortings)
        {
            <li><code>@columnSorting.ColumnId</code> — @(columnSorting.Ascending ? "ascending" : "descending")</li>
        }
    </ul>
}
else
{
    <p><em>No active sort.</em></p>
}

<SimpleTable Items="s_items"
             RowClickSelectionEnabled="false"
             @bind-SortingState="_boundSortingState"
             MaximumHeight="300px">
    <Columns>
        <SimpleTableTemplateColumn Id="Key" Title="Key" SortExpression="x => x.Key" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>
                @context.Key
            </CellContent>
        </SimpleTableTemplateColumn>
        <SimpleTableTemplateColumn Id="Value" Title="Value" SortExpression="x => x.Value" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>
                @context.Value
            </CellContent>
        </SimpleTableTemplateColumn>
        <SimpleTableTemplateColumn Id="Timestamp" Title="Timestamp" SortExpression="x => x.Timestamp" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>
                @context.Timestamp
            </CellContent>
        </SimpleTableTemplateColumn>
    </Columns>
</SimpleTable>

<h3>Change the sort from outside — <code>SetSortingStateAsync</code></h3>

<DescriptionBanner>
    Hold the table in an <code>@("@ref")</code> and call <code>SetSortingStateAsync</code> for the changes a
    binding expresses badly — a reset, a jump to a preset — and for callers that are not in a render at all.
    Unlike the parameter the method has no memory of what it was handed before, so resetting works here even
    though the empty sorting is a value the table has seen before.
</DescriptionBanner>

<div class="options">
    <div class="option">
        <label>Sort order</label>
        <Button Text="Reset sort" OnClick="ResetSortingAsync" Size="ButtonSize.Small" />
    </div>
</div>

@if (_pushedSortingState.ColumnSortings.Count > 0)
{
    <ul>
        @foreach (var columnSorting in _pushedSortingState.ColumnSortings)
        {
            <li><code>@columnSorting.ColumnId</code> — @(columnSorting.Ascending ? "ascending" : "descending")</li>
        }
    </ul>
}
else
{
    <p><em>No active sort.</em></p>
}

<SimpleTable @ref="_pushTargetTable"
             Items="s_items"
             RowClickSelectionEnabled="false"
             SortingStateChanged="PushedSortingStateChanged"
             MaximumHeight="300px">
    <Columns>
        <SimpleTableTemplateColumn Id="Key" Title="Key" SortExpression="x => x.Key">
            <CellContent>
                @context.Key
            </CellContent>
        </SimpleTableTemplateColumn>
        <SimpleTableTemplateColumn Id="Value" Title="Value" SortExpression="x => x.Value">
            <CellContent>
                @context.Value
            </CellContent>
        </SimpleTableTemplateColumn>
        <SimpleTableTemplateColumn Id="Timestamp" Title="Timestamp" SortExpression="x => x.Timestamp">
            <CellContent>
                @context.Timestamp
            </CellContent>
        </SimpleTableTemplateColumn>
    </Columns>
</SimpleTable>
